      "text": "<|content_text|>Every evening at dusk, Elias sat at the scarred pine desk and opened the leather-bound log, its pages already thick with his cramped, precise handwriting. He recorded the barometric pressure, the color of the sea, the precise minute the lamp was lit, and the names of any vessels that passed within sight of the tower. For twenty years, the ritual had been a kind of prayer, a way of asserting order against the chaos of the Atlantic, but lately the entries seemed to belong to a stranger\u2014someone who had never felt the particular dread of a foghorn that sounded too close, or the way the stairs seemed to lengthen when he climbed them alone.\n\nThe distrust began with a discrepancy he could not resolve: on the night of the fourteenth, he had written that the weather was clear and the sea calm, yet he woke with salt crusted in his hair and a bruise blooming on his forearm that he could not explain. He turned back through the pages and found other lies, or perhaps truths he had refused to see\u2014an entry describing a ship that had never arrived, a notation of a visitor\u2019s signature that looked, upon closer inspection, like his own hand written backward. The log was no longer a record of his vigil; it was a mirror that showed him a keeper who was more diligent, more sane, and more alone than he had ever been.\n\nNow he writes with a trembling hand, pausing after each sentence to compare the ink to the reality outside the window, wondering if the book is slowly replacing him with a more perfect version of himself. He has begun to leave gaps\u2014blank spaces where a storm should be described, or a crossed-out line that reads only *do not trust the light*\u2014and he suspects that the lighthouse itself might be using his own words to keep him anchored to the rock. The log sits open on the desk like a second keeper",
