      "text": "<|content_text|>In a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer, each token is processed by a sparse subset of \u201cexpert\u201d feed-forward networks rather than by one monolithic FFN.\n\n**How routing works**\nA lightweight router (a linear layer) takes the token\u2019s hidden state and outputs a score for every expert. The layer then selects the top-$k$ experts (commonly $k=1$ or $2$) for that token. The token is forwarded through only those $k$ experts; their outputs are weighted by the router\u2019s softmax probabilities (often with a load-balancing loss to prevent collapse) and summed to produce the final representation.\n\n**Cost of top-$k$ selection**\nThe router\u2019s score computation is $O(d \\cdot E)$ per token ($d$ = hidden dim, $E$ = number of experts). Selecting the top-$k$ is $O(E \\log k)$, which is negligible compared to the $O(d^2)$ cost of a full FFN. The real savings come from sparsity: only $k$ experts are activated, so the layer\u2019s compute is roughly $k/E$ of a dense layer of the same total capacity. The overhead is mainly memory (storing all expert weights) and communication (in distributed settings, tokens must be routed to the GPUs holding the selected experts).\n\n**Why shared experts are used**\nShared (or \u201ccommon\u201d) experts are always active for every token, alongside the routed top-$k$ experts. They serve two purposes:\n1. **Stability and knowledge preservation:** They provide a consistent baseline transformation, preventing the model from losing general capabilities when routing is noisy or imbalanced.\n2. **Mitigating routing failure:** If the router assigns a token poorly, the shared experts still process it correctly. This reduces the risk of \u201cdead\u201d experts and improves training stability, allowing the",
      "best": {
